---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const layers = [
  {
    title: "Passwords",
    points: [
      "At least 16 characters. Length beats complexity rules, and a password manager makes it free.",
      "Hashed with PBKDF2-SHA256 at 100,000 iterations, salted and mixed with a secret key kept apart from the database. Sign-in takes the same time whether or not the account exists, so response timing can't be used to discover who has one.",
      "Error messages never say which half was wrong.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Sessions",
    points: [
      "A short-lived access token, held in memory only and never written to your browser's storage, so a script bug can't lift a long-lived credential.",
      "A long-lived refresh token in an HttpOnly cookie that page code can't read. It rotates on every use.",
      "Present an already-used refresh token and the whole session is revoked. That's what a stolen token looks like.",
      "Every write that relies on the cookie also needs a CSRF token the cookie alone can't supply.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Email codes",
    points: [
      "On by default: signing in with a password also asks for a 6-digit code sent to your email. It works once and expires after 10 minutes.",
      "Tick \"Trust this device for 30 days\" and that browser skips the code until then. Signing out everywhere or changing your password forgets every trusted device.",
      "An authenticator app or a passkey replaces the email code, if you'd rather not wait for mail.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Passkeys",
    points: [
      "Sign in with your fingerprint, face or device PIN, from a button or straight from the email field's autofill.",
      "Only the public key is stored. A database copy gives an attacker nothing to sign in with.",
      "Your browser binds every passkey to app.pacestreak.com, so a look-alike site can't use it. That's why a passkey counts as two-factor on its own.",
      "Adding or removing one needs your password, so a stolen session can't plant its own way in. Each challenge works once.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Authenticator app",
    points: [
      "Optional: time-based codes from any authenticator app, with the secret encrypted at rest.",
      "A code can't be replayed, even within its own 30-second window.",
      "Ten single-use recovery codes, replaceable at any time. Each works in place of any code.",
      "Turning two-factor on or off needs your password and a current code.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "Your control",
    points: [
      "A list of every signed-in device. End one, or sign out everywhere.",
      "Changing your password keeps this session and ends all the others. Resetting it ends every session.",
      "Lost your password and your email? A recovery code sets a new password, once, and ends every session.",
      "Changing your email needs your password and only takes effect when you enter the code sent to the new address. The old address is told.",
      "A security history of sign-ins, password, passkey and two-factor changes, and an email alert when a device you've never used signs in.",
      "Rate limits on sign-up, sign-in, two-factor, recovery, password reset and verification, and failed attempts watched by IP and by account.",
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Security and privacy — PaceStreak"
  description="How PaceStreak protects accounts and training data: 16-character passwords, email codes at sign-in, optional authenticator apps and passkeys, recovery codes, rotating refresh tokens, nightly backups and no third parties."
  path="/security"
>
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        Training data is health data. We treat it that way.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted text-pretty">
        None of this is novel. It's the careful, boring version of each
        decision, written down here so you know what to expect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-line bg-bg-alt py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        layers.map((layer) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">{layer.title}</h2>
            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3">
              {layer.points.map((p) => (
                <li class="tick text-[0.96rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">{p}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl prose-page">
      <h2>No third parties, enforced</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, no tracking pixels, no advertising, no font CDN, no
        embedded widgets, on this site, on the blog or in the app. Every site
        ships a content security policy of <code>default-src 'self'</code>, so
        the browser refuses to load anything from anywhere else. If someone
        added a tracker by mistake, it wouldn't run.
      </p>

      <h2>Email that stays quiet</h2>
      <p>
        No newsletters, no marketing email. PaceStreak only writes about your
        account and your own activity, and every kind of email except
        sign-in codes and security notices can be turned off in the app. A habit's name, note or
        amount never appears in an email subject or a push notification's
        title, because those show on a lock screen; details stay inside.
      </p>

      <h2>Backups</h2>
      <p>
        The database can be restored to any minute of the last seven days, and
        a full copy is taken off-site every night, so one provider's bad day
        can't take your history with it.
      </p>

      <h2>One domain, one trust boundary</h2>
      <p>
        The session is shared across <code>pacestreak.com</code> so the app can
        reach the API. That makes a promise necessary:{" "}
        <strong>nothing untrusted is ever hosted under this domain</strong>. No
        user-generated pages, no third-party tools on a subdomain.
      </p>

      <h2>Your data, your call</h2>
      <p>
        Export everything as JSON, CSV or a calendar file, whenever you like.
        Delete your account and it's scheduled 30 days out, so a mistake is
        recoverable, then removed. Body metrics are private and never leave
        your account.
      </p>

      <h2>Source code</h2>
      <p>
        Every line of PaceStreak is open source under AGPL-3.0 at{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/PaceStreak">github.com/PaceStreak</a>, so
        you can check each measure on this page against the code. Its security
        rests on those measures, not on anything being hidden. Uptime is public
        at <a href="https://status.pacestreak.com">status.pacestreak.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
      <p>
        Please email <a href="mailto:hello@pacestreak.com">hello@pacestreak.com</a>{" "}
        rather than opening a public issue. You'll get an acknowledgement within
        72 hours and a straight answer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
